{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00227 - MONDO_grouped_11393_17773_32766",
  "verbalization": "Lovastatin is implicated in the pathogenesis of Hypoalphalipoproteinemia through a mechanism involving the induction of Fenofibrate. The evidence suggests that Lovastatin, through its indication for Familial Hyperlipidemia, leads to the use of Fenofibrate, which in turn is associated with Hypoalphalipoproteinemia. This relationship is supported by multiple pathways, all of which involve the same intermediate step of Fenofibrate. The shared indication for Fenofibrate across different forms of hyperlipidemia indicates a potential common mechanism, possibly involving lipid metabolism or the regulation of lipoprotein levels. The presence of multiple pathways with similar intermediate steps supports the hypothesis that Lovastatin may influence lipid metabolism in a way that indirectly affects lipoprotein levels, leading to Hypoalphalipoproteinemia. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of Fenofibrate as a key mediator in the relationship between Lovastatin and the observed condition.",
